WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.425
A Use Case diagram explains which actors interact with a
system and which capabilities the system offers.

00:00:07.545 --> 00:00:13.920
It is a view of scope and responsibility, rather than a
sequence of internal implementation steps.

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:20.415
Our customer portal lets customers submit and track
requests, while a support agent resolves them.

00:00:20.535 --> 00:00:28.210
We will create objects, inspect their identity and
appearance, add relationships and notes, and compare
include with extend.

00:00:28.330 --> 00:00:31.780
Keep each capability meaningful to an actor.

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:38.750
Use Insert and Use case to add Read help.

00:00:38.870 --> 00:00:44.520
A good name describes an actor's goal rather than a screen
widget or a database table.

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:49.890
Review the kind, name and alias in the creation screen
before adding the object.

00:00:50.010 --> 00:00:55.235
The alias is useful when you refer to this capability
repeatedly in source.

00:00:55.355 --> 00:00:59.730
After creation, locate the declaration and the rendered
object.

00:00:59.850 --> 00:01:06.750
We will connect capabilities separately so that adding an
object is not confused with defining who can use it.

00:01:10.267 --> 00:01:16.067
An actor represents a role interacting with the system,
not necessarily one named person.

00:01:16.187 --> 00:01:19.237
Add an Auditor role to this practice model.

00:01:19.357 --> 00:01:25.857
Inspect the actor and use-case kind choices rather than
selecting a shape only for decoration.

00:01:25.977 --> 00:01:31.727
External services can also be actors when they play a
relevant role at the system boundary.

00:01:31.847 --> 00:01:40.722
Keep the diagram focused on the capabilities you are
explaining, and avoid listing every internal
implementation component as another actor.

00:01:44.233 --> 00:01:47.833
Select Submit request and inspect its properties.

00:01:47.953 --> 00:01:55.978
Name and alias identify the capability, while color,
stereotype and the business-object option change how it is
presented.

00:01:56.098 --> 00:02:00.673
The container setting places it within a system boundary
or package.

00:02:00.793 --> 00:02:06.393
Keep identity changes deliberate because other statements
refer to these objects.

00:02:06.513 --> 00:02:13.588
A boundary groups what belongs to the modeled system; it
is not a substitute for explaining the capability itself.

00:02:13.708 --> 00:02:17.583
Review the source when moving an object between
containers.

00:02:21.100 --> 00:02:25.650
Relationship tools connect the actors and capabilities you
have modeled.

00:02:25.770 --> 00:02:31.345
Open the relationship creation screen and inspect the
endpoint and type choices.

00:02:31.465 --> 00:02:38.890
Associations explain participation, while generalization
and other relationship types express different meanings.

00:02:39.010 --> 00:02:45.110
Select endpoints deliberately and reconnect a relationship
when the original endpoint was wrong.

00:02:45.230 --> 00:02:50.180
Do not compensate for an incorrect association with a
misleading label.

00:02:50.300 --> 00:02:55.900
Notes can explain an assumption, but the relationship
should still be semantically correct.

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:04.775
Paste the supplied reference example and compare its two
additional relationships.

00:03:04.895 --> 00:03:09.670
Submit request includes validation as part of that
capability.

00:03:09.790 --> 00:03:15.315
Attach a file extends the submission behavior under an
appropriate optional condition.

00:03:15.435 --> 00:03:21.060
Include and extend are not interchangeable labels for any
arrow between two ovals.

00:03:21.180 --> 00:03:28.755
Use them when you can explain the behavioral distinction
in plain language, and check arrow direction and
stereotype in the source.

00:03:28.875 --> 00:03:35.350
Keep the actor associations visible so the diagram still
answers who needs each capability.

00:03:38.867 --> 00:03:44.917
Use packages or boundaries to organize the model, and
notes to record useful assumptions.

00:03:45.037 --> 00:03:50.462
Diagram settings control overall presentation, including
layout choices.

00:03:50.582 --> 00:03:55.107
Open these screens and compare their purpose with the
object inspector.

00:03:55.227 --> 00:04:02.227
A good system boundary keeps internal capabilities
together while actors remain outside the system they use.

00:04:02.347 --> 00:04:05.947
Layout and styling should reinforce that distinction.

00:04:06.067 --> 00:04:14.217
If the valid syntax you need is not represented by a
dedicated form, preserve it in source and inspect the
resulting preview.

00:04:17.733 --> 00:04:23.333
Open the Use Case diagram settings and change the layout
direction to top to bottom.

00:04:23.453 --> 00:04:27.203
Give this review copy an explicit title and caption.

00:04:27.323 --> 00:04:34.823
These settings commit as you leave the fields; this
inspector does not require the Apply button used by some
other editors.

00:04:34.943 --> 00:04:39.493
Inspect the saved title and caption statements and the
current preview.

00:04:39.613 --> 00:04:46.513
Use shared Undo to reconsider a committed setting rather
than assuming that closing the inspector cancels it.

00:04:50.033 --> 00:04:56.058
Select Submit request and Track request together with a
modifier, then inspect the bulk panel.

00:04:56.178 --> 00:05:00.303
Apply a supported shared style and check the source
changes.

00:05:00.423 --> 00:05:07.973
A common style can make related capabilities easier to
recognize, but retain clear names and meaningful
boundaries.

00:05:08.093 --> 00:05:16.043
Copy or duplicate only a verified selection, and review
resulting identifiers and associations before continuing.

00:05:16.163 --> 00:05:22.388
Shared history lets you reconsider a bulk operation
without manually reversing each object.

00:05:25.900 --> 00:05:31.200
Pause and add an Auditor actor and a Read help capability
to a practice copy.

00:05:31.320 --> 00:05:35.595
Decide whether they belong in an association and explain
why.

00:05:35.715 --> 00:05:42.490
Add one useful note, then use the reference example to
explain include and extend in your own words.

00:05:42.610 --> 00:05:48.460
Inspect a capability's container and apply a shared style
to two selected objects.

00:05:48.580 --> 00:05:56.680
Your success check is that someone can identify the system
boundary, the actors and their goals without needing to
know the implementation.

00:06:00.200 --> 00:06:04.425
A capability model should make system scope
understandable.

00:06:04.545 --> 00:06:09.445
Keep actors, use cases, boundaries and relationship
meanings distinct.

00:06:09.565 --> 00:06:18.215
Inspect identity and placement before presentation, and
verify associations after moving, renaming or duplicating
objects.

00:06:18.335 --> 00:06:26.860
The next lesson describes the types and members inside the
system, using a Class diagram to answer a different
question from this actor-centered view.
